WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:32.000
Welcome. Welcome.

00:32.000 --> 00:35.000
Howdy. Well, now, ain't that handy?

00:35.000 --> 00:36.000
It's bad, pal.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
We in the road takes a bend in here, runs right by the front door.

00:40.000 --> 00:44.000
No, Mr. Clam, but this is your driveway. It goes with the house.

00:44.000 --> 00:46.000
Is this thing a hire?

00:46.000 --> 00:50.000
Oh, yes, indeed. It was built by John Barrymore, one of our finest actors.

00:50.000 --> 00:53.000
Pretty fair stonemason, too.

00:54.000 --> 00:58.000
Hey, Jed! Is here as dandy soil?

00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
Fine, Granny. We'll commence plowing tomorrow.

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
But this is Beverly Hill. Dirt is dirt.

01:05.000 --> 01:06.000
Yes, I know, but...

01:07.000 --> 01:09.000
Why don't we look around inside, eh?

01:14.000 --> 01:15.000
Well, here we are.

01:16.000 --> 01:19.000
I hope you're going to like this place I picked out for you.

01:19.000 --> 01:22.000
Maybe it's twice the larger than what you're used to.

01:22.000 --> 01:25.000
But I feel a man with 25 million dollars in my bank

01:25.000 --> 01:29.000
should live in a manner that... Come in, come in. This is your home.

01:44.000 --> 01:46.000
As I say, it may be a bit more than you're used to,

01:46.000 --> 01:48.000
but I'm sure you'll be comfortable.

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
Now, my secretary will be over right away to help you with the hiring of servants

01:51.000 --> 01:53.000
or whatever problems you may have.

01:53.000 --> 01:55.000
She's a very efficient girl, Miss Hathaway.

01:55.000 --> 01:58.000
Jed! Jed!

02:01.000 --> 02:02.000
Jed?

02:03.000 --> 02:08.000
You and Jed are fetching my stove so I can get some vittles to cook in it?

02:08.000 --> 02:10.000
Oh, you have a beautiful stove.

02:11.000 --> 02:13.000
Thank you. Fetch it in, Jed.

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
You scare up some wood, Ellie.

02:15.000 --> 02:18.000
No, no. I mean you have a stove already installed in the kitchen.

02:19.000 --> 02:20.000
Where's that?

02:20.000 --> 02:21.000
I'll show you.

02:21.000 --> 02:23.000
Excuse us.

02:25.000 --> 02:28.000
Ellie, you go with Granny. She'll still need you to fetch some wood.

02:28.000 --> 02:30.000
Sure, Papa.

02:33.000 --> 02:37.000
Uncle Jed! Guess what? There's a whole nother house up here.

02:40.000 --> 02:42.000
Guess who'll come down from there?

02:42.000 --> 02:44.000
Like it's not that belonged to somebody else.

02:45.000 --> 02:46.000
John!

02:48.000 --> 02:49.000
This is your kitchen.

02:49.000 --> 02:51.000
This is your kitchen.

02:53.000 --> 02:55.000
And here's the stove.

02:56.000 --> 02:58.000
The last word in food preparation.

02:59.000 --> 03:01.000
And speak to me.

03:01.000 --> 03:05.000
You'll find everything you could possibly want stored right in here.

03:05.000 --> 03:06.000
See?

03:08.000 --> 03:09.000
Huh?

03:11.000 --> 03:15.000
Well, I'll leave you ladies to your culinary delights.

03:16.000 --> 03:17.000
Au revoir.

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
Bon appetit.

03:21.000 --> 03:22.000
Talk's gibberish.

03:22.000 --> 03:26.000
You've set a great deal of stone this year's stove, Granny.

03:29.000 --> 03:31.000
Well, we'll see.

03:35.000 --> 03:38.000
Ellie, you run out and fetch some wood.

03:38.000 --> 03:41.000
I'll get a fire going in here and we'll see.

03:41.000 --> 03:44.000
Yeah, girl. I hope it's all right for you to be missing school.

03:44.000 --> 03:46.000
Oh, sure, Uncle Jed.

03:46.000 --> 03:48.000
They ain't straight from the fifth grade.

03:49.000 --> 03:50.000
You're in the fifth grade already?

03:50.000 --> 03:52.000
Starting this year.

03:53.000 --> 03:57.000
Don't seem like no time at all since you went with your ma to get you started in the first grade.

03:57.000 --> 04:01.000
You was six years old. No bigger than that.

04:02.000 --> 04:05.000
You drove clean over to Oxford.

04:05.000 --> 04:08.000
So you could go to the same school your pa went to.

04:08.000 --> 04:11.000
You wouldn't know that town today, Uncle Jed.

04:11.000 --> 04:12.000
Oxford?

04:12.000 --> 04:13.000
Yes, sir.

04:13.000 --> 04:20.000
Boy, that town has grown and grown and spread out and built up and just kept getting bigger and bigger.

04:20.000 --> 04:21.000
Where today?

04:21.000 --> 04:25.000
Well, I bet you there must be at least three or four dozen people living there.

04:26.000 --> 04:30.000
It tastes like that's all right to visit, but I wouldn't want to live there.

04:31.000 --> 04:33.000
Do you have any assistance?

04:33.000 --> 04:35.000
Don't thank you, Mr. Drysdale.

04:35.000 --> 04:37.000
We were just talking about Jethro here getting back to school.

04:37.000 --> 04:42.000
Oh, well, we have some excellent educational facilities out here, Mr. Pappett.

04:42.000 --> 04:43.000
Where do you go to school, Jethro?

04:43.000 --> 04:44.000
Oxford.

04:46.000 --> 04:49.000
Yeah, you see, Jethro's ma, that's my cousin Pearl.

04:49.000 --> 04:54.000
She married an Oxford man, so naturally Jethro, he goes to school there too.

04:54.000 --> 04:56.000
I'm the champion crawdad eater.

04:56.000 --> 04:57.000
And Oxford?

04:58.000 --> 05:00.000
Jethro put away three buckets of crawdad.

05:00.000 --> 05:01.000
Four.

05:02.000 --> 05:04.000
And he's learned to cipher and everything.

05:04.000 --> 05:06.000
Cipher some for Mr. Drysdale.

05:08.000 --> 05:10.000
One and one's two.

05:10.000 --> 05:12.000
Two and two's four.

05:12.000 --> 05:14.000
Four and four's eight.

05:16.000 --> 05:19.000
I have to take off my shoes to get any property back.

05:19.000 --> 05:22.000
I'm sure we're thinking of two different Oxford's.

05:22.000 --> 05:24.000
You see, there's one that's very famous and very old.

05:24.000 --> 05:26.000
That's where Jethro goes to school.

05:26.000 --> 05:28.000
Been there since pioneer days.

05:28.000 --> 05:29.000
That's how come it got its name.

05:29.000 --> 05:32.000
It's where the oxen used to ford the reverend.

05:32.000 --> 05:37.000
So the folks took to calling it Oxenford later on and got shortened to Oxford.

05:37.000 --> 05:39.000
Well, I'll be running.

05:39.000 --> 05:41.000
Oh, by the way, here are your keys.

05:41.000 --> 05:43.000
My secretary should be over very soon.

05:43.000 --> 05:47.000
Well, thank you very much, Mr. Drysdale, for everything you've been doing for us.

05:47.000 --> 05:49.000
Oh, it's my pleasure, Mr. Clavitt.

05:49.000 --> 05:51.000
You see, I'm not only your banker, I'm your next door neighbor.

05:53.000 --> 05:56.000
Well, you see, now ain't that nice.

05:56.000 --> 06:00.000
Well, now why don't you bring your missus over tonight for supper

06:00.000 --> 06:03.000
and I'll have Granny Womp Womp and Miss Gritzenhauld jowl.

06:03.000 --> 06:06.000
Well, you see, right now my wife isn't boss.

06:06.000 --> 06:08.000
Well, that's too bad. When should she be back?

06:08.000 --> 06:10.000
Not too soon, Fawcett.

06:11.000 --> 06:12.000
I'll see you later.

06:12.000 --> 06:13.000
Thank you, Mr. Drysdale.

06:13.000 --> 06:14.000
Long.

06:16.000 --> 06:22.000
Uncle Jed, you reckon one of these days I can have me a little bitty old taste of Granny's moonshine?

06:22.000 --> 06:26.000
Ah, Jethro, you know the family rule about Granny's moonshine?

06:26.000 --> 06:30.000
You get your first taste on your wedding day and not before.

06:30.000 --> 06:33.000
Well, Uncle Jed, that might be an awful long time.

06:33.000 --> 06:35.000
I ain't even got me no sweetheart.

06:35.000 --> 06:36.000
How come?

06:36.000 --> 06:37.000
Well, I'm back home.

06:37.000 --> 06:42.000
Every time I get me a girl, one of them there big fellas will take her right away from me.

06:42.000 --> 06:44.000
Maybe out here in Beverly Hills.

06:44.000 --> 06:46.000
Paul, could I please have the axe?

06:46.000 --> 06:48.000
I gotta split some wood for Granny.

06:48.000 --> 06:49.000
Fetch it, Jethro.

06:49.000 --> 06:51.000
Ain't this place got a woodpile?

06:51.000 --> 06:57.000
Not that I can find, but I found a whole row of black old dead tree trunks

06:57.000 --> 07:00.000
pretty near as high as this height.

07:00.000 --> 07:02.000
Did you kind of dig for you to tackle?

07:02.000 --> 07:03.000
Well, shucks, no, Paul.

07:03.000 --> 07:05.000
They're only this big around.

07:05.000 --> 07:08.000
And all trimmed and topped and strung together with a black rope

07:08.000 --> 07:12.000
so as no matter which one you cut, the others keep it from falling.

07:13.000 --> 07:15.000
Somebody had a right smart idea there.

07:15.000 --> 07:16.000
It's the same.

07:16.000 --> 07:19.000
I think I'd rather have Jethro do the cutting.

07:19.000 --> 07:21.000
Oh, falling me?

07:21.000 --> 07:26.000
Ah, cutting trees and chopping logs ain't no work for a girl.

07:26.000 --> 07:28.000
I'm just as strong as Jethro.

07:28.000 --> 07:30.000
You ain't neither.

07:30.000 --> 07:31.000
I am so?

07:31.000 --> 07:32.000
You ain't neither.

07:32.000 --> 07:33.000
I am so too.

07:33.000 --> 07:34.000
You want a wrestle?

07:34.000 --> 07:35.000
Done too.

07:36.000 --> 07:37.000
Now, here, here.

07:37.000 --> 07:39.000
That ain't no fair fight.

07:39.000 --> 07:40.000
Somebody go get hurt.

07:41.000 --> 07:43.000
Now, yeah, you see, you're getting too rough.

07:43.000 --> 07:44.000
You can't throw.

07:45.000 --> 07:47.000
A person can't get crippled from a hold like that.

07:47.000 --> 07:48.000
Get up from there.

07:49.000 --> 07:50.000
I can't up there, Jethro.

07:51.000 --> 07:52.000
You turn him loose.

07:52.000 --> 07:53.000
You get up from there, Paul.

07:53.000 --> 07:56.000
You take the strap to the booth.

07:58.000 --> 08:01.000
Now, here, you get out there and chop down one of them tree trunks.

08:01.000 --> 08:03.000
And saw Granny some nice logs.

08:03.000 --> 08:05.000
Yes, sir, I'm prepared.

08:09.000 --> 08:11.000
Could have walked you if you hadn't tripped me.

08:11.000 --> 08:13.000
Come back here and sit down.

08:13.000 --> 08:14.000
I want to talk to you.

08:15.000 --> 08:18.000
Put them fire in square and I'll deal with them kids.

08:18.000 --> 08:19.000
Uh, you won't.

08:20.000 --> 08:22.000
That's what I want to talk to you about.

08:22.000 --> 08:23.000
Ellie Mae.

08:26.000 --> 08:28.000
You're getting too big to wrestle with boys.

08:28.000 --> 08:30.000
I ain't as big as Jethro.

08:31.000 --> 08:34.000
Big that way, I mean, uh, growed up.

08:34.000 --> 08:35.000
You're a young lady now.

08:36.000 --> 08:40.000
You gotta start finding your manners and fixing your stuff up real nice.

08:40.000 --> 08:41.000
And wearing dresses.

08:42.000 --> 08:45.000
Paul, folks would call me a sissy.

08:45.000 --> 08:48.000
Well, then you're sissy for girls to act like girls.

08:48.000 --> 08:49.000
You see, Ellie.

08:49.000 --> 08:52.000
I raised you like a boy and I was wrong to do it.

08:53.000 --> 08:55.000
I reckon every man like to have a son.

08:55.000 --> 08:56.000
You were my only youngin'.

08:57.000 --> 09:01.000
And when your ma passed away, I decided to turn you into a boy.

09:02.000 --> 09:05.000
By the time Granny come to help out, you was too wild to tame.

09:06.000 --> 09:10.000
I thunder you fit out, run out, climb out, fight and out.

09:10.000 --> 09:11.000
Shoot every boy in them hills.

09:12.000 --> 09:13.000
I still can, pa.

09:15.000 --> 09:16.000
It ain't fittin'.

09:16.000 --> 09:18.000
It ain't right for folks to go against nature.

09:18.000 --> 09:19.000
I look at old Duke.

09:20.000 --> 09:22.000
Reg, we could turn him into a cat.

09:22.000 --> 09:23.000
Course not.

09:26.000 --> 09:27.000
That's right.

09:27.000 --> 09:29.000
Nature made him into a dog.

09:30.000 --> 09:32.000
Same way nature made you into a girl.

09:33.000 --> 09:36.000
And lately she's getting more and more positive about it.

09:38.000 --> 09:39.000
You mean my gaius is grown?

09:40.000 --> 09:41.000
No, nothing like that.

09:42.000 --> 09:43.000
You're pretty.

09:43.000 --> 09:45.000
Oh, pa.

09:45.000 --> 09:47.000
I know you don't like it when I say that,

09:47.000 --> 09:51.000
but you like it when the young fellas around here come in saying it, and they will.

09:51.000 --> 09:55.000
Only they'll probably be using words, fancy words I won't even understand.

09:56.000 --> 09:57.000
But Ellie, me?

09:57.000 --> 10:01.000
Ain't nobody can ever tell you how pretty you really are, see?

10:02.000 --> 10:04.000
You're the living picture of your mama.

10:04.000 --> 10:08.000
You're still here? I thought you were going up to the Clab of the State.

10:08.000 --> 10:11.000
I didn't think your food was supposed to be out since simultaneously.

10:11.000 --> 10:14.000
I'm sorry, I was just wondering if there was any food in the kitchen.

10:14.000 --> 10:15.000
I'll get it.

10:17.000 --> 10:19.000
I'd like to see if there's anything in the kitchen.

10:20.000 --> 10:21.000
I'll go and get it.

10:23.000 --> 10:24.000
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

10:25.000 --> 10:27.000
I didn't mean to get you in trouble.

10:27.000 --> 10:28.000
I'm sorry.

10:29.000 --> 10:30.000
I'm sorry.

10:30.000 --> 10:31.000
I'm sorry.

10:31.000 --> 10:34.000
I didn't think your food was supposed to be out since simultaneously.

10:34.000 --> 10:35.000
In the event of crises...

10:35.000 --> 10:39.000
The only crises you have to worry about right now is keeping Mr. J.D. Clampett happy.

10:39.000 --> 10:40.000
Now get up there.

10:40.000 --> 10:42.000
Tell me, how did you like the flamingos?

10:43.000 --> 10:44.000
What flamingos?

10:44.000 --> 10:48.000
I thought pink flamingos around the swimming pool would add a rather elegant touch.

10:49.000 --> 10:51.000
Mr. Clampett is not a man educated with elegance.

10:51.000 --> 10:52.000
That will take time.

10:52.000 --> 10:54.000
Right now, there are more pressing problems.

10:54.000 --> 10:57.000
Of course, getting settled, the servant problem.

10:57.000 --> 10:59.000
Well, whatever they are, just get up there and solve them all.

10:59.000 --> 11:01.000
Now J.D. Clampett is this bank's largest depositor.

11:01.000 --> 11:04.000
And I'm making his satisfaction your responsibility.

11:05.000 --> 11:08.000
I accept the mantle of responsibility with which you have cloaked my shoulders.

11:09.000 --> 11:14.000
And I shall so conduct myself, that if this great financial institution shall last a thousand years...

11:16.000 --> 11:18.000
This was their finest hour.

11:21.000 --> 11:23.000
Hey, Andy Wood, all right?

11:23.000 --> 11:26.000
Got a lot of pitch and tar in it. It ought to burn real good.

11:26.000 --> 11:27.000
Yeah.

11:27.000 --> 11:29.000
That flimsy grate holds up.

11:30.000 --> 11:33.000
Granny, this is top of that tree trunk.

11:33.000 --> 11:35.000
You want I should split it up to it?

11:35.000 --> 11:37.000
No, just leave it outside.

11:37.000 --> 11:38.000
Yes, sir.

11:39.000 --> 11:41.000
This place ain't even got a wood box.

11:41.000 --> 11:43.000
Yeah, folks don't need much wood out here.

11:43.000 --> 11:46.000
Remember what Pearl said, it don't get cold.

11:46.000 --> 11:47.000
Yeah, I remember.

11:47.000 --> 11:52.000
It might not get cold in the day, but it sure freezes solid at night.

11:52.000 --> 11:53.000
How do you know?

11:53.000 --> 11:57.000
I show you how I know.

11:59.000 --> 12:02.000
Ever been a fool in this here storage bin as frozen?

12:02.000 --> 12:04.000
Harder than a rock.

12:04.000 --> 12:05.000
Yes.

12:05.000 --> 12:13.000
Yes.

12:17.000 --> 12:19.000
Might have thin skinned hog.

12:24.000 --> 12:26.000
But I'll be dogged, huh?

12:26.000 --> 12:35.000
Take me two days to thaw out that ham, for I can every cook it.

12:35.000 --> 12:40.000
People ought to know better than store food up against the north wall.

12:40.000 --> 12:43.000
Tomorrow we'll dig us a root cellar and we'll put all this stuff in it.

12:43.000 --> 12:44.000
Get going.

12:44.000 --> 12:46.000
Go on out there and catch us a chicken.

12:46.000 --> 12:48.000
Okay, Uncle Jester.

12:48.000 --> 12:50.000
How do you know we got chickens?

12:50.000 --> 12:53.000
At least this size is fine to have chickens.

12:53.000 --> 12:56.000
In your frost.

13:24.000 --> 13:27.000
I keep a chicken.

13:30.000 --> 13:32.000
Here's that bucket, you want it?

13:32.000 --> 13:34.000
You take it, Ellie, see if you can find a creek.

13:34.000 --> 13:36.000
I gotta have some water.

13:36.000 --> 13:38.000
A little hump on this please?

13:38.000 --> 13:40.000
It ain't even got a well.

13:40.000 --> 13:41.000
Uncle Jed, Uncle Jed.

13:41.000 --> 13:43.000
Yeah, boy, did you find a chicken?

13:43.000 --> 13:44.000
I found something.

13:44.000 --> 13:47.000
I ain't sure if it's a chicken, a goose, a duck or a fox.

13:47.000 --> 13:48.000
Where is it?

13:48.000 --> 13:49.000
I couldn't catch it.

13:49.000 --> 13:51.000
That thing can outrun a jack rabbit.

13:51.000 --> 13:53.000
There ain't no goose or duck.

13:53.000 --> 13:55.000
It must be a chicken.

13:55.000 --> 13:58.000
If it is, Ma was right about things growing bigger out here.

13:58.000 --> 14:00.000
That thing was this high.

14:00.000 --> 14:01.000
Chicken.

14:01.000 --> 14:03.000
Out you through.

14:03.000 --> 14:05.000
Honest, it's got legs this long.

14:05.000 --> 14:07.000
Dibbies on the drumstick.

14:07.000 --> 14:08.000
The drumstick ain't much.

14:08.000 --> 14:11.000
Whoever gets that neck is eating from now on.

14:11.000 --> 14:14.000
Commences here and runs plum up to here.

14:14.000 --> 14:15.000
Yes, sir.

14:15.000 --> 14:16.000
Okay, Ma.

14:16.000 --> 14:19.000
You ain't forgot what I said about Granny Jed, will you?

14:19.000 --> 14:21.000
I ain't been to Granny's Jug.

14:21.000 --> 14:23.000
You have, you're in for it.

14:23.000 --> 14:25.000
Honest, across my heart.

14:25.000 --> 14:27.000
Where'd you see this chicken?

14:27.000 --> 14:29.000
Down by the cement pond.

14:29.000 --> 14:31.000
Cement pond?

14:31.000 --> 14:35.000
Uncle Jed, that pond is the fanciest thing you ever did see.

14:35.000 --> 14:40.000
Five day's steps, so the cattle can walk right down into it and get a drink.

14:40.000 --> 14:44.000
And up at one end, there's a lady standing there made out of rock.

14:44.000 --> 14:48.000
And she's pouring water out of the jar right into that cement pond.

14:48.000 --> 14:50.000
Oh.

14:50.000 --> 14:54.000
Well, that's how come that big old pink chicken to get away from me.

14:54.000 --> 14:59.000
Flew right over the top of that there rock lady, landed in that pond and swam like an otter.

14:59.000 --> 15:03.000
Just a-lapping them big old pink wings of Howard.

15:03.000 --> 15:06.000
What color did you see that chicken in the morning?

15:06.000 --> 15:08.000
It's pink.

15:08.000 --> 15:13.000
Ain't I told you that stuff would stunt your gross?

15:13.000 --> 15:15.000
I'm not describing it. I didn't. I didn't touch a drop.

15:15.000 --> 15:17.000
You and me is going to the woodshed.

15:17.000 --> 15:19.000
Now, hold it.

15:19.000 --> 15:21.000
Jethro, you swear to be telling the truth.

15:21.000 --> 15:24.000
So help me, Jefferson Davis.

15:24.000 --> 15:27.000
You're half off when you speak of the president.

15:27.000 --> 15:30.000
But I ain't the president no more.

15:30.000 --> 15:34.000
I'll have no Yankee talk in my kitchen.

15:34.000 --> 15:39.000
Now, Jethro, you and me is going back out there and corner that chicken.

15:39.000 --> 15:41.000
Danny, you get your fire to going.

15:41.000 --> 15:45.000
Ellie Mae, you sweep up out front and keep your eyes open for that Miss Hathaway.

15:45.000 --> 15:48.000
Count on her to take you in here and get you in the right kind of clothes.

15:48.000 --> 15:50.000
Come on.

15:52.000 --> 15:54.000
Pink chicken.

16:05.000 --> 16:07.000
You there, young man.

16:07.000 --> 16:11.000
I ain't a young man, but I sure do thank you for thanking, sir.

16:11.000 --> 16:13.000
Good heavens, you're a girl.

16:13.000 --> 16:17.000
I'm Jane Hathaway, executive secretary to Mr. Drysdale.

16:17.000 --> 16:19.000
And you, I take it, are a domestic of some sort.

16:19.000 --> 16:21.000
Clean girl, housemaid.

16:21.000 --> 16:23.000
Just what are your duties with Mr. Clapper?

16:23.000 --> 16:25.000
Well, he sent me out here to sweep up.

16:25.000 --> 16:28.000
But he said you would take me in hand and get me in the right kind of clothes and everything.

16:28.000 --> 16:29.000
Indeed I shall.

16:29.000 --> 16:32.000
We have a complete servant's wardrobe from chef to chauffeur.

16:32.000 --> 16:34.000
Come with me.

16:36.000 --> 16:41.000
What in the name of Thomas Chippendale is this?

16:41.000 --> 16:45.000
Mr. Clapper, seeing as this is disgraceful and unsightly amount of debris.

16:45.000 --> 16:47.000
Oh, yes ma'am.

16:47.000 --> 16:49.000
This is all his.

16:50.000 --> 16:54.000
Oh, what charming antiques.

16:54.000 --> 16:59.000
Just like he said, you throw a rock lady pouring water into a cement pond.

16:59.000 --> 17:01.000
Yes, sir.

17:01.000 --> 17:04.000
And over there is where I seen that great big pink chicken.

17:04.000 --> 17:07.000
Only thing is it don't sound like a chicken.

17:07.000 --> 17:11.000
It sounds like it's a kind of hollering ho.

17:22.000 --> 17:27.000
I reckon you'd make a hollering noise too if you was to lay an egg like that.

17:27.000 --> 17:29.000
I ain't an old man.

17:29.000 --> 17:31.000
That's what I call an egg.

17:31.000 --> 17:34.000
Then you won't have to worry about food now.

17:34.000 --> 17:37.000
Good food.

17:43.000 --> 17:46.000
That lame city stove don't draw worth nothing.

17:46.000 --> 17:48.000
Granny, look at this egg.

17:48.000 --> 17:50.000
What's all the smoke?

17:50.000 --> 17:52.000
Stove don't work.

17:53.000 --> 17:55.000
Well, it's just a stuffed up flu.

17:55.000 --> 17:56.000
Ain't got a flu.

17:56.000 --> 17:58.000
Ain't even got a stove pipe.

17:58.000 --> 18:00.000
I'll get the shovel and a hoe and rake it out of there.

18:00.000 --> 18:04.000
You keep this egg.

18:12.000 --> 18:14.000
Just like everything else out here.

18:14.000 --> 18:18.000
Fro solid.

18:21.000 --> 18:24.000
Really, my dear, you look quite charming.

18:24.000 --> 18:27.000
Well, I feel like a dad playing foe.

18:28.000 --> 18:29.000
Just a moment.

18:29.000 --> 18:31.000
He's up now. I'll be right back.

18:32.000 --> 18:34.000
Oh, that was my Pa.

18:34.000 --> 18:35.000
I take it he's a gardener.

18:35.000 --> 18:36.000
Oh, yes, ma'am.

18:36.000 --> 18:39.000
Pa just can't wait to commence gardening.

18:39.000 --> 18:40.000
Perfect.

18:40.000 --> 18:44.000
However, as though I admire his enthusiasm, I must forbid him access through the main entrance.

18:44.000 --> 18:47.000
Now, tell me about the rest of the staff.

18:47.000 --> 18:49.000
Is there a chauffeur?

18:49.000 --> 18:50.000
A what?

18:50.000 --> 18:52.000
Someone who does the driving.

18:52.000 --> 18:53.000
Oh, yes, ma'am.

18:53.000 --> 18:54.000
That's Jethro.

18:54.000 --> 18:57.000
Of course, I don't reckon he can stay around very long.

18:57.000 --> 19:00.000
He's got to get back to Oxford and go to school.

19:00.000 --> 19:02.000
Oxford?

19:02.000 --> 19:05.000
He attends Oxford University?

19:05.000 --> 19:07.000
Well, he just calls it school.

19:07.000 --> 19:10.000
Pa went to school back too.

19:10.000 --> 19:13.000
Oh, yes. Old school time.

19:13.000 --> 19:16.000
Great pride, those Oxford men.

19:16.000 --> 19:18.000
Sorry I couldn't stop before.

19:18.000 --> 19:20.000
Granny was having trouble in the kitchen.

19:20.000 --> 19:22.000
Pa, do I have to wear this year's stuff and mishap?

19:22.000 --> 19:23.000
No, no.

19:23.000 --> 19:26.000
There can be no appeal for Mr. Clampett's orders.

19:26.000 --> 19:27.000
Thank you, ma'am.

19:27.000 --> 19:29.000
Bad to see you taking the firm, eh?

19:29.000 --> 19:30.000
And me?

19:30.000 --> 19:32.000
Brad Furtie.

19:32.000 --> 19:33.000
Makes you look taller.

19:33.000 --> 19:36.000
It's got me walking on pegs.

19:36.000 --> 19:37.000
Yeah.

19:37.000 --> 19:38.000
Well, run in and show Granny.

19:38.000 --> 19:44.000
And get rid of those old clothes, dear.

19:44.000 --> 19:46.000
Granny, I presume, is the cook.

19:46.000 --> 19:48.000
Yes, ma'am, but she ain't too happy about it right now.

19:48.000 --> 19:50.000
I shall deal with her directly.

19:50.000 --> 19:52.000
Now, let's see.

19:52.000 --> 19:55.000
Maid, gardener, cook, chauffeur.

19:55.000 --> 19:58.000
Oh, uh, I understand Jethro is an Oxford man.

19:58.000 --> 20:00.000
You bet he is.

20:00.000 --> 20:02.000
I'm, uh, quite anxious to meet him.

20:02.000 --> 20:03.000
Don't blame you.

20:03.000 --> 20:05.000
Fine looking young feller.

20:05.000 --> 20:06.000
Single too.

20:06.000 --> 20:08.000
And he's on the lookout for a girl.

20:08.000 --> 20:11.000
I am only interested in the intellectual Rob Ford,

20:11.000 --> 20:14.000
which I would naturally have with an Oxford man.

20:14.000 --> 20:17.000
I presume he went to Eaton as a boy.

20:17.000 --> 20:18.000
Fine old Jethro.

20:18.000 --> 20:21.000
He went to Eaton the minute he was born.

20:21.000 --> 20:22.000
Yes, of course.

20:22.000 --> 20:25.000
I suppose his father matriculated him.

20:25.000 --> 20:28.000
I kind of think maybe it was his mom.

20:28.000 --> 20:29.000
Oh, no, no matter.

20:29.000 --> 20:32.000
At any rate, he's an Eaton man.

20:32.000 --> 20:33.000
That he is.

20:33.000 --> 20:37.000
Matter of fact, Jethro won the Eaton championship.

20:37.000 --> 20:39.000
A-mur-velous.

20:39.000 --> 20:40.000
What was he champion of?

20:40.000 --> 20:41.000
Eaton.

20:41.000 --> 20:44.000
Yes, I know, but what was it?

20:44.000 --> 20:45.000
Cricket?

20:45.000 --> 20:47.000
Oh, no, no, it was craw-dash.

20:47.000 --> 20:51.000
I don't think even Jethro would eat crickets.

20:51.000 --> 20:54.000
I was referring to the game.

20:54.000 --> 20:56.000
Dad, where's the axe?

20:56.000 --> 20:57.000
I'll fetch it, Granny.

20:57.000 --> 20:59.000
You talk to Miss Hathaway.

20:59.000 --> 21:02.000
And just why does Cook need an axe?

21:02.000 --> 21:04.000
I've hit this with everything I could lay a hand to.

21:04.000 --> 21:06.000
I even bonked it with an iron skiff.

21:06.000 --> 21:11.000
You are supposed to hit it with a croquet mallet.

21:11.000 --> 21:13.000
All right, where is it?

21:13.000 --> 21:15.000
I don't think I'll tell you.

21:15.000 --> 21:18.000
There is a time for work and a time for play.

21:18.000 --> 21:21.000
Now then, what are we cooking for Mr. Clapper?

21:21.000 --> 21:23.000
Well, I don't know what you're cooking him,

21:23.000 --> 21:27.000
but if he wants any vittles from me, somebody better shoot a possum.

21:27.000 --> 21:28.000
Possum?

21:28.000 --> 21:30.000
You got a better idea?

21:30.000 --> 21:33.000
Of course, a nice big fluffy souffle.

21:33.000 --> 21:36.000
All right, you shoot it and you skin it.

21:36.000 --> 21:39.000
I don't even know what a souffle is.

21:39.000 --> 21:41.000
What kind of a cook are you?

21:41.000 --> 21:44.000
I'm a cook with a stove that don't draw,

21:44.000 --> 21:46.000
food that's froze solid,

21:46.000 --> 21:50.000
chickens that can't be caught, eggs that can't be broke,

21:50.000 --> 21:53.000
and a smart Alecky City woman telling me my business.

21:53.000 --> 21:56.000
That's what kind of a cook I am.

21:56.000 --> 21:58.000
Mind your tongue, little woman.

21:58.000 --> 22:00.000
I can have your job.

22:00.000 --> 22:02.000
You sure can, and the sooner the better.

22:02.000 --> 22:05.000
Jack, don't you come in here. Just wait till you see what he's got.

22:05.000 --> 22:09.000
Thank heaven. Now I can have an intelligent conversation.

22:09.000 --> 22:11.000
Look, Granny, I got that bird jacket.

22:11.000 --> 22:13.000
I hope it ain't as tough as its eggs.

22:13.000 --> 22:15.000
But that is a flamingo.

22:15.000 --> 22:18.000
Oh, no, ma'am, that's my nephew, Jethro.

22:18.000 --> 22:20.000
Get close, I'm gonna load him his hathaway.

22:20.000 --> 22:22.000
I didn't have him.

22:22.000 --> 22:25.000
Ain't this the big dangest chicken you ever did, say?

22:25.000 --> 22:28.000
Did you go to school at Oxford?

22:28.000 --> 22:30.000
Yes, ma'am, I'm in the fifth grade.

22:30.000 --> 22:32.000
Stop ragging and rig that chicken's neck.

22:32.000 --> 22:34.000
Ellie, babe!

22:34.000 --> 22:36.000
No, stop, you boy-belly.

22:36.000 --> 22:38.000
Don't harm one feather of that beautiful bird.

22:38.000 --> 22:40.000
I've taken just about as much as I'm gonna take from you.

22:40.000 --> 22:42.000
Yeah, Granny.

22:42.000 --> 22:44.000
Ellie, you get the fire going under the big kettle outdoors.

22:44.000 --> 22:46.000
Young lady, where is your maid's uniform?

22:46.000 --> 22:47.000
I ain't gone away.

22:47.000 --> 22:48.000
Oh, yes, you are.

22:48.000 --> 22:49.000
No, I ain't.

22:49.000 --> 22:51.000
Oh, yes, you are, if I must subdue you forcefully.

22:51.000 --> 22:53.000
I wouldn't try to.

22:53.000 --> 22:55.000
Oh, you fat dog!

22:55.000 --> 22:57.000
Fat dog!

22:57.000 --> 22:58.000
I'll tell you.

22:58.000 --> 23:00.000
You trust me.

23:01.000 --> 23:03.000
I'll settle him, Ellie.

23:03.000 --> 23:05.000
You get the fire going.

23:05.000 --> 23:09.000
Here, here, Granny, come on, let her up, get off of me.

23:09.000 --> 23:12.000
You are just stars.

23:12.000 --> 23:15.000
All of you, sacked, fired.

23:15.000 --> 23:19.000
You are the most incompetent, insubordinate, voluptuous group of domestics.

23:19.000 --> 23:21.000
It has never been my misfortune.

23:21.000 --> 23:22.000
Just have the way.

23:22.000 --> 23:25.000
Oh, chief, you're just in time to help me take disciplinary action.

23:25.000 --> 23:26.000
You're fired.

23:26.000 --> 23:27.000
I've already told them.

23:27.000 --> 23:28.000
Goddamn you.

23:28.000 --> 23:30.000
These people are the clampets.

23:30.000 --> 23:32.000
Clampets?

23:32.000 --> 23:33.000
Yes, that's right.

23:33.000 --> 23:35.000
Now go back to the office and pick up your severance pay.

23:35.000 --> 23:37.000
You are through.

23:37.000 --> 23:39.000
Now, Mr. Drysdale, hold on a spell.

23:39.000 --> 23:42.000
I reckon there's been a kind of a misunderstanding.

23:42.000 --> 23:45.000
But this little lady's been a right big help to us.

23:45.000 --> 23:49.000
Why, she's fitted just like one of the family.

23:49.000 --> 23:53.000
Matter of fact, her and me is going out hunting.

23:53.000 --> 23:55.000
Ain't that right, Miss Hathaway?

23:55.000 --> 23:57.000
Yes, indeed.

23:58.000 --> 23:59.000
Yep.

24:00.000 --> 24:04.000
Jethro, you can pipe that pink chicken back out of the cement pond.

24:04.000 --> 24:08.000
We're going out and shooting us a nice, big, fluffy, soup-like.

24:08.000 --> 24:09.000
Yeah.

24:38.000 --> 24:42.000
